	"web_text": "Kira Dominguez Hultgren is a United States–based artist and educator. With a dual degree (MFA/MA) in fine arts and visual and critical studies from California College of the Arts, she creates artwork that critiques traditional understandings of material culture and interrogates her own cultural heritage, self-described as “Chicanx, Punjabi, Hawaiian.” She pushes the boundaries of weaving with dimensional sculptures that employ materials sourced from the cultures with which she identifies. In this artwork, made on a vertical loom, she utilizes silk from her grandmother’s salwar clothing, rope from her favorite climbing gym in Utah, and found wood, plastic, and other materials to question the Chicana writer and activist Cherríe Moraga’s theory of “garbled utterances” (fragmented, or even unspoken, communication). As Dominguez Hultgren describes, “So often when I speak about identity, family history, race, I feel like I’m choking up the words, caught in a tension of too many nuances, held by the fear that I know I’m doing it all wrong . . . But when telling a story through my body at the loom, through fingers, found materials, weaving . . . I can touch it, handle it, in a way that words and their definitions don’t allow.” The loom also serves as a figurative scaffold for the artist’s familial stories: “the stories that one generation had to forget and another generation had to remember; both generations weaving into one another.”",
